# Multisite & Tenant Isolation (ADR-022)

Each hall is one Multisite site with its own per-site table set
(`bc_{blog_id}_*`). Identity (`bc_users`) and network registry are global.
For Portal integration within the single authoritative Bingo Innovation Portal
environment, immutable Portal Hall identity `halls.id` maps to WordPress Site
ID; names and domains are never cross-system identity (ADR-035/BR-072). The
network mapping repository and synchronized hall-local replicas are implemented.

## Context Resolution

- Resolve the current hall from the site: `get_current_blog_id()`.
- NEVER trust a `blog_id` / `hall_id` / site identifier from user input, URL
  params, or request bodies without verifying authorization for THAT site.
- An unknown/unmapped hostname must NEVER fall back to another hall's site.

## Data Access

- Hall-owned data (products, orders, customers, tickets, sessions)
  lives in per-site tables — native isolation. Do not add `blog_id` columns to
  per-site tables; the table prefix IS the isolation.
- ADR-031's `bingo_game_types`, `bingo_play_products`, `bingo_packages`,
  `bingo_package_components`, `bingo_package_addons`, `bingo_sessions`, and
  `bingo_session_packages` are all hall-owned per-site tables. Append these
  names to `$wpdb->prefix`; do not add another `bc_` sub-prefix or a redundant
  `blog_id`. ADR-044/BR-079 approve `bingo_package_addons` and
  `PackageAddonService` for Catalog v6, but they are not implemented; the
  current schemas implement the other six baseline tables.
- Package add-on service/admin requests derive Hall context only from the
  current WordPress site. Never accept a request Hall/blog/site ID. Resolve
  Package, Play Product, relationship and WooCommerce product identities only
  through the verified current Hall's prefixed tables/APIs. An ID absent from
  the current Hall returns not found. If Hall A and Hall B contain the same
  numeric ID, a Hall A request resolves only Hall A's entity and must never read,
  expose, validate against, modify or return Hall B's entity. A request-supplied
  Hall/blog/site ID cannot change that namespace. Super Admin must first enter
  the intended Hall context, and multi-Hall switching must not retain table
  names, rows, projection counts or cache state from the previous site.
- Tenant-isolation tests must prove both namespace cases: an ID present only in
  Hall B returns not found from Hall A; and when the same numeric ID exists in
  both Halls, a Hall A request accesses only Hall A's row and leaves Hall B byte-
  identical.
- The Package add-on form always displays the current Package's configured
  relationships, even when disabled or currently ineligible, but offers a Play
  Product without a relationship only when it is active, medium-compatible and
  resolved inside the current Hall. Never expose another Hall's candidate or
  configured Play Product through IDs, labels, prefill, errors or retained UI
  state.
- Package add-on projection retry accepts the current Hall's
  `bingo_play_product_id` and internally resolves its linked current-Hall
  WooCommerce product. Never accept a request-supplied WooCommerce product ID as
  reconciliation authority. Increment 1 defers the Hall-wide mismatch scanner;
  any later scanner must remain current-Hall scoped and cannot become a normal
  cross-Hall Manager surface.
- The additive Session schema's `bingo_portal_session_templates`,
  `bingo_session_schedules`, `bingo_session_schedule_dates`, and
  `bingo_session_schedule_packages` are likewise hall-owned/per-site and use
  the current site's `$wpdb->prefix`. They implement approved ADR-043;
  production deployment is not claimed. Together with
  the six implemented baseline tables, they make ten implemented tables total.
- Portal-authoritative Game Types may be replicated only into the correctly
  mapped Hall's per-site Game Type table. Only Portal Sessions owned by that
  Portal Hall, satisfying `bingo_sessions.hall_id == mapped halls.id`, are
  eligible for its Commerce v1 sync; received/shared Sessions and
  `allSessions()` semantics must not cross this tenant boundary. Portal Session
  `days` must not drive scheduling, generate occurrences, or constrain/validate
  Commerce scheduling in v1. Future advisory validation requires a separately
  approved requirement (ADR-035/BR-072/BR-073).
- A custom table's scope follows data ownership (ADR-022): hall-owned /
  tenant-owned operational data is per-site unless a documented decision
  requires network-wide storage. Genuinely network-wide records (e.g.
  billing status, audit log per ADR-023, reporting facts) may use
  `$wpdb->base_prefix` and require an indexed `blog_id`/`site_id` when
  tenant attribution exists, with every query against them filtering by it
  unless the caller is a Platform Admin. A future Android/tablet device
  registry's scope is not yet decided — do not classify it as network-wide
  or per-site ahead of the upcoming Android/API persistence design.
- `switch_to_blog()` only when cross-hall access is explicitly required AND
  the user is Platform Admin; always pair with `restore_current_blog()`
  (try/finally). Cross-hall writes require an audit log entry.
- Network-activated plugins must not assume single-site behavior.

## Identity Model (locked)

- One network user per email — the same credentials work at every hall (BR-007).
- Per-hall data stays separate: orders, order history, carts, payment tokens.
- Addresses (BR-028): a registered customer's previously used addresses MAY be
  suggested/reused across hall storefronts to reduce data entry, but each hall
  stores its OWN copy. Updating an address in one hall must NEVER rewrite
  another hall's stored customer or order data.
- The customer-scoped address suggestion above is the ONLY sanctioned
  cross-hall read for non-platform users: read-only, limited to the
  authenticated customer's own address records. Every other cross-hall read
  requires Platform Admin capability + audit log.
- Staff may belong to multiple sites; the hall switcher lists only sites the
  user is explicitly authorized for (BR-019).

## Testing Requirement

Any feature touching hall data must include a test asserting Hall A cannot
read or modify Hall B's products, orders, customers, media, reports, payment
settings, tickets, or devices.
